\text{Total males who don't listen} &= \text{Total} - \text{Total females who don't listen} \\
&= 150 - 102 \\
&= 48 \\
\end{align*}

We also know that the total number of males surveyed is 110 (150 total surveyed - 40 females surveyed). Therefore, the number of males who don't listen equals the total number of males surveyed minus the number of males who listen:

\begin{align*}
\text{Total males who don't listen} &= \text{Total males surveyed} - \text{Males who listen} \\
&= 110 - 62 \\
&= 48 \\
\end{align*}

So, the number of females who listen to the station is 68. </think> <answer> 68 </answer>

ground_truth:
Consider a triangle $ABC$ where $a = b.$

[asy]
unitsize (3 cm);

pair A, B, C;

A = (0,0);
B = (2,0);
C = (1,0.2);

draw(A--B--C--cycle);

label("$A$", A, W);
label("$B$", B, E);
label("$C$", C, N);
label("$a$", (B + C)/2, N);
label("$a$", (A + C)/2, N);
label("$c$", (A + B)/2, S);
[/asy]

As $\angle ACB$ approaches $180^\circ,$ $c$ approaches $2a,$ so $\frac{a^2 + b^2}{c^2}$ approaches $\frac{a^2 + a^2}{(2a)^2} = \frac{1}{2}.$  This means $M \le \frac{1}{2}.$

On the other hand, by the triangle inequality, $c < a + b,$ so
